# Caddy in front of Parrothecary. Install to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
#   sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# Two reasons this exists, and neither is performance. The barcode scanner needs
# a secure context, so the phones have to reach the app over HTTPS or the camera
# is simply unavailable. And the login rate limit counts by IP, which means
# something has to decide what a request's IP actually is.

# The address the phones use. An IP rather than a name, because the house has no
# local DNS to invent a name in — the router hands out addresses and nothing
# resolves anything. A bookmark or a home-screen shortcut means nobody types it
# twice.
#
# The `https://` is required. Caddy does not turn on automatic HTTPS for a bare
# IP address, so without the scheme this quietly serves plain HTTP and the
# barcode scanner stops working — the camera needs a secure context.
#
# `tls internal` means Caddy is its own certificate authority: it issues a
# certificate for this address from a root it generated. No public authority will
# certify a private address, and this one never appears on the internet.
#
# Each phone has to trust that authority once, or every visit is a warning page:
#   sudo cat /var/lib/caddy/.local/share/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt
# and install it on the device.
#
# To move to a name later — say `parrothecary.home.arpa` via a DNS rewrite in
# AdGuard Home — change this one line and reload. The phones keep trusting the
# same root, so the certificate they already accepted stays valid; only the
# certificate Caddy issues from it changes. Nothing has to be reinstalled.
#
# If the container's address ever changes, this line changes with it. That is
# why it is static and outside the router's DHCP range.
#
#
# REACHING IT FROM OUTSIDE THE HOUSE
#
# A second address goes on the same line, comma separated:
#
#   https://192.168.0.5, https://<vpn-name-of-the-host> {
#
# The reason is the certificate, not convenience. A VPN reaches the app by a
# different name, and a certificate issued for `192.168.0.5` does not cover it —
# so the phone shows a warning, and a browser that distrusts the certificate
# turns the camera off. The barcode scanner stops working, which is a strange
# symptom to trace back to an address.
#
# Naming both here gets a valid certificate for each, from the same authority the
# phones already trust. Nothing is reinstalled on the phones; Caddy issues the
# second certificate on reload.
#
# What sits in front of this, on the machine hosting the VPN, is a forwarding
# rule aimed at the container:
#
#   iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -d <vpn-address-of-the-host> \
#     -p tcp --dport 443 -j DNAT --to-destination 192.168.0.5:443
#
# Two things about that rule, both learned the hard way. It must name a
# destination: unrestricted, it would catch every outbound request to port 443
# passing through that machine, which is most of the internet. And port 80 wants
# the same treatment, or typing the bare name reaches nothing — the browser tries
# port 80, finds no forwarding, and hangs. Caddy redirects 80 to 443 by itself
# once the request arrives.
#
# Whether a matching POSTROUTING/MASQUERADE rule is needed depends on the VPN.
# Some do their own address translation, in which case a reply finds its way back
# unaided; without it the container answers a request that appears to come from
# an address its own router has never heard of, and the page hangs rather than
# failing. Test before adding one: hanging is the symptom either way, so the
# quick check is whether the request ever arrives at all.
https://192.168.0.5 {
	tls internal

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
		# Overwritten, not appended, and this is the whole point.
		#
		# Caddy's default is to add the client's address to whatever
		# X-Forwarded-For the request already carried. The app reads the first
		# entry, so a request that arrives with a header of its own would decide
		# its own identity — and the login rate limit counts per IP, so anybody
		# could have as many attempts as they liked by changing that value
		# between tries.
		#
		# {remote_host} is the address Caddy is actually talking to. Setting it
		# replaces anything the client sent.
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
	}

	# The app sets no-store on the things that matter (exports, backups, photos),
	# and the pages themselves are rendered per request.
	#
	# Caddy compresses text-ish content types only, so the backup zip and the box
	# photographs — both already compressed — should pass through untouched. That
	# is Caddy's own default rather than something set here, so if downloads ever
	# feel slow on the phone, this line is the first thing to check.
	encode gzip

	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/parrothecary.log
		# Names of medication are not in the paths, but box and product ids are.
		# Keep these on the machine rather than shipping them anywhere.
	}
}

# Nothing else is served. The app binds to 127.0.0.1, so port 3000 is not
# reachable from the network even if this file is wrong.
